---
import NoticePage from '../components/NoticePage.astro';

// The site's second page, and its first legal-notice surface. The layout, the `noindex` and the
// style are `NoticePage`'s, shared with `/privacy`.
---

<NoticePage title="Attribution — Stacks" description="Where this shelf's book data comes from.">
      <h1>Attribution</h1>

      <p>
        Book data on this shelf comes from four catalogues. Nothing here is
        affiliated with any of them.
      </p>

      <h2>Google</h2>
      <p>
        Book data from Google Books. The <em>powered by Google</em> graphic is
        displayed on the shelf itself, and every book Google contributed to
        carries a link back to its Google Books page.
      </p>

      <h2>Apple</h2>
      <!--
        ⚠️ **Two sentences, where #106 decided one — and the condition it
        attached is what fired.**

        §7.1's governing sentence is "List only the trademarks actually used in
        your materials." Its three published variants are all scoped to the
        *badge*; the card locked the **icon**, for which Apple publishes no
        variant, so #106 dropped the Apple-logo sentence — and wrote the
        condition out in as many words: *"If the vendored icon artwork turns out
        to carry the Apple logo, that sentence returns; that is a check on the
        asset, not a reopened decision."*

        The mark that shipped is the Apple logo silhouette. So it returns.
      -->
      <p>
        The Apple logo is a trademark of Apple Inc., registered in the U.S. and
        other countries. Apple Books is a service mark of Apple Inc.
      </p>

      <h2>Ask the library</h2>
      <p>How the Ask the library box treats your questions is on the <a href="/privacy">privacy page</a>.</p>

      <h2>Credits</h2>
      <!--
        Manners rather than compliance: neither Open Library nor O'Reilly
        requires attribution that any source read has found. O'Reilly is named
        although its mark is never rendered — it is a contributor the vault
        records, and a credits line is prose rather than a link, so the 403 its
        URL returns does not reach it.
      -->
      <p>
        Book metadata and cover art from Open Library, Google Books, Apple Books
        and O&rsquo;Reilly.
      </p>

      <!--
        The site has no navigation, no header link and nothing that has ever gone
        anywhere, so a browser back button is not a way back for someone who
        arrived here directly.
      -->
      <p class="back"><a href="/">Back to the shelf</a></p>
</NoticePage>
